[Result 2, p. 155, unchanged] RESULT 2: Suppose there exist 2 vertices $i \in V_R$ and $j \in V_R$ such that $d_i > d_i^r$, $d_j > d_j^r$, and $Z_i 
eq Z_j$. Then $h$ is not point identified. [Proof, corrected: the starting pair is constructed, so that the cross edges $\{i, u_2\}$ and $\{j, u_1\}$ are absent from it (P012-E003); $h$ is written with its two arguments (P012-N006)] Suppose the observed RDS data are $G_R$, $\mathbf{Z}_R$, $\mathbf{t}_R$, $\mathbf{d}_R$, and there exist distinct $i \in V_R$ and $j \in V_R$ with $d_i^r < d_i$, $d_j^r < d_j$, and $Z_i 
eq Z_j$. Without loss of generality, suppose $Z_i = 0$ and $Z_j = 1$. Let $B = \{\{k, l\} : (k, l) \in E_R\}$ and $s_v = d_v - d_v^r \geq 0$ for $v \in V_R$. Let $G^1_{SU}$ be the graph whose vertices are those of $V_R$ and, for each $v \in V_R$, $s_v$ new unsampled vertices joined only to $v$, and whose edges are those of $B$ and the edges just described. Let $u_1$ be one of the new neighbours of $i$ and $u_2$ one of the new neighbours of $j$, and let $\mathbf{Z}^1_{SU}$ extend $\mathbf{Z}_R$ with $Z^1_{u_1} = 0$, $Z^1_{u_2} = 1$ and any binary traits on the other new vertices. Then: (a) $(G^1_{SU}, \mathbf{Z}^1_{SU}) \in \mathrm{C}(G_R, \mathbf{d}_R, \mathbf{Z}_R)$, $\{i, u_1\} \in E^1_{SU}$, $\{j, u_2\} \in E^1_{SU}$, $\{i, u_2\} 
otin E^1_{SU}$ and $\{j, u_1\} 
otin E^1_{SU}$. Let $(G^2_{SU}, \mathbf{Z}^2_{SU})$ be identical to $(G^1_{SU}, \mathbf{Z}^1_{SU})$ except that $\{i, u_1\}$ and $\{j, u_2\}$ are removed and $\{i, u_2\}$ and $\{j, u_1\}$ are added. Then: (b) $(G^2_{SU}, \mathbf{Z}^2_{SU}) \in \mathrm{C}(G_R, \mathbf{d}_R, \mathbf{Z}_R)$, and the two pairs have the same vertices, the same degree at every vertex, the same number of edges $|E_{SU}|$ and the same traits. (c) With $Q = \sum_{k \in V_{SU}} d_k Z_k$, the denominator of (1) is the same for both pairs and equals $$D = \sum_{k,l \in V_{SU}} (d_k \delta_{kl} - d_k d_l/2|E_{SU}|) Z_k Z_l = Q -  rac{Q^2}{2|E_{SU}|} =  rac{Q\,(2|E_{SU}| - Q)}{2|E_{SU}|} > 0,$$ because $Q \geq d_j > 0$ and $2|E_{SU}| - Q \geq d_i > 0$. (d) [(A1)] With $h_1 = h(G^1_{SU}, \mathbf{Z}^1_{SU})$ and $h_2 = h(G^2_{SU}, \mathbf{Z}^2_{SU})$, $$h_1 - h_2 =  rac{2\left(1 -  rac{d_j d_{u_2}}{2|E_{SU}|}ight) - 2\left(0 -  rac{d_j d_{u_2}}{2|E_{SU}|}ight)}{D} =  rac{2}{D} 
eq 0.$$ Therefore homophily is not point identified. [Exact instance, from the validator] $V_R = \{1, 2\}$, $E_R = \{(1, 2)\}$, $\mathbf{d}_R = (2, 2)$, $\mathbf{Z}_R = (0, 1)$, $i = 1$, $j = 2$, $u_1 = 3$, $u_2 = 4$ with traits $0, 1$: $E^1_{SU} = \{\{1, 2\}, \{1, 3\}, \{2, 4\}\}$, $E^2_{SU} = \{\{1, 2\}, \{1, 4\}, \{2, 3\}\}$, $|E_{SU}| = 3$, $Q = 3$, $D = 3/2$, $h_1 = 1/3$, $h_2 = -1$, $h_1 - h_2 = 4/3 = 2/D$.
